    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Science (Biology) · Chapter 13</span>
      <h1>Our Environment: Ecosystems, Food Webs, Ozone &amp; Waste Management</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Comprehensive revision guide covering biotic/abiotic interactions, Lindeman's 10% energy transfer rule, biomagnification of DDT, CFC-induced stratospheric ozone depletion, and modern eco-friendly waste management with 20 board MCQs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1: Ecosystems & Trophic Levels -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>1. Ecosystem Structure &amp; Energy Flow</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌿 Ecosystem: Biotic &amp; Abiotic Components</h3>
          <p class="formula">Ecosystem = Biotic Community (Living) + Abiotic Habitat (Physical Environment)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Natural vs. Artificial Ecosystems:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Natural:</em> Forests, ponds, lakes, oceans, deserts (self-sustaining without human intervention).
              <br/>• <em>Artificial (Man-made):</em> Crop fields, gardens, aquariums (require constant human maintenance and energy input).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Biotic Functional Groups:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Producers (Autotrophs):</em> Green plants and photosynthetic cyanobacteria that trap solar energy to manufacture organic glucose via photosynthesis.
              <br/>• <em>Consumers (Heterotrophs):</em> Primary consumers (herbivores: deer, cattle), secondary consumers (carnivores: frog, fox), tertiary consumers (apex predators: tiger, eagle).
              <br/>• <em>Decomposers:</em> Microscopic saprophytic bacteria and fungi that secrete extracellular enzymes to digest dead organic remains, returning essential inorganic mineral ions back to the soil and air.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Ecological Importance of Decomposers:</strong> Without decomposers, nutrient cycling would halt, biogeochemical cycles would lock up, and dead organismal waste would pile up endlessly.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ Lindeman's 10% Energy Law &amp; Unidirectional Flow</h3>
          <p class="formula">Energy Available at Next Trophic Level = 10% of Previous Level (90% dissipated as heat &amp; metabolism)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Initial Solar Capture:</strong> Green terrestrial plants capture merely <strong>~1%</strong> of the total incident solar energy falling upon their leaves and convert it into photochemical food energy.</li>
            <li><strong>The 10% Law (Raymond Lindeman, 1942):</strong> Only 10% of the energy consumed by a trophic level is converted into new body biomass available for consumption by the next higher level. The remaining 90% is dissipated as thermal metabolic heat (respiration, movement, digestion, excretion) into the environment.</li>
            <li><strong>Why Food Chains Are Limited to 3–4 Levels:</strong> Because energy diminishes exponentially by 90% across every trophic transfer, the residual energy at the 4th or 5th trophic level becomes so minuscule that it cannot sustain viable populations of higher carnivores.</li>
            <li><strong>Strictly Unidirectional Energy Flow:</strong> Unlike mineral nutrients which cycle cyclically through decomposers, energy captured from the sun cannot revert backwards from carnivores to herbivores or producers.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <!-- SVG Diagram 1: Energy Pyramid & 10% Law -->
      <div class="diagram-wrapper">
        <div class="diagram-header">
          <h4>Figure 1: Trophic Level Energy Pyramid &amp; Raymond Lindeman's 10% Law</h4>
          <span class="badge">Visual Concept</span>
        </div>
