    <!-- Section 3: Waste Management -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>3. Waste Management: Biodegradable vs. Non-Biodegradable</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🗑️ Biodegradable vs. Non-Biodegradable Waste</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Biodegradable Waste:</strong> Substances that can be broken down into non-toxic simpler substances by biological action of microorganisms (bacteria &amp; fungi) through enzymatic digestion.
              <br/>• <em>Examples:</em> Fruit &amp; vegetable peels, paper, cow dung, cotton, wool, fallen leaves.
              <br/>• <em>Enzymatic Specificity:</em> Bacteria possess specific enzymes that act only on specific chemical linkages (like cellulose or starch glycosidic bonds).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Non-Biodegradable Waste:</strong> Substances that cannot be broken down by microorganisms because decomposers lack enzymes capable of breaking synthetic polymeric bonds.
              <br/>• <em>Examples:</em> Plastic bottles, polythene bags, nylon, glass, aluminum foil, DDT, electronic waste (e-waste).
              <br/>• <em>Ecological Hazards:</em> Choke city drainage systems, kill grazing cows that ingest plastics, cause microplastic contamination of groundwater, persist for centuries.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>♻️ Eco-Friendly Solid Waste Disposal Methods</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. Segregation at Source:</strong> Separating wet organic waste (green bin) from dry recyclable waste (blue bin) at homes and schools before collection.</li>
            <li><strong>2. Composting &amp; Vermicomposting:</strong> Converting kitchen scraps and garden leaves into nutrient-rich organic manure using aerobic bacteria or earthworms (<em>Eisenia fetida</em>).</li>
            <li><strong>3. Biogas (Gobar Gas) Plants:</strong> Anaerobic microbial digestion of animal dung and organic sewage produces clean combustible methane gas ($CH_4$) and nutrient-rich slurry fertilizer.</li>
            <li><strong>4. Incineration:</strong> Controlled high-temperature (over 1000°C) combustion used specifically for toxic biomedical and hospital waste (syringes, dressings) to convert them into sterile ash.</li>
            <li><strong>5. Sanitary Landfills:</strong> Non-recyclable solid trash compressed and layered below ground with impermeable leachate barriers to prevent hazardous chemical runoff into groundwater aquifers.</li>
            <li><strong>6. Disposable Cups Trade-off (Board Classic Question):</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Plastic cups:</em> Non-biodegradable and environmental hazard.
              <br/>• <em>Clay cups (Kulhads):</em> Biodegradable but stripping massive fertile topsoil on an industrial scale damaged agricultural topsoil.
              <br/>• <em>Disposable paper cups:</em> Environmentally superior alternative, being both biodegradable and recyclable.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

